---
import Guide from '@/layouts/Guide.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import SignalFlow from '@/components/SignalFlow.astro'

const answerTs = `// Your chat endpoint: return the trace id with the answer.
app.post('/api/chat', async (req, res) => {
  let traceId = ''
  const answer = await mcpd.withSpan('chat_turn', async (run) => {
    traceId = run.traceId
    return await answerQuestion(req.body.message)
  })
  res.json({ answer, traceId })
})`

const answerPy = `# Your chat endpoint: return the trace id with the answer.
@app.post("/api/chat")
def chat(body: ChatRequest):
    with mcpd.trace("chat_turn") as run:
        answer = answer_question(body.message)
        return {"answer": answer, "trace_id": run.trace_id}`

const buttons = `<div class="feedback" data-trace-id="TRACE_ID_FROM_THE_ANSWER">
  <button type="button" data-vote="up">Helpful</button>
  <button type="button" data-vote="down">Not helpful</button>
</div>

<script>
  document.addEventListener('click', async (e) => {
    const button = e.target.closest('[data-vote]')
    if (!button) return
    const box = button.closest('.feedback')
    await fetch('/api/feedback', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ traceId: box.dataset.traceId, vote: button.dataset.vote }),
    })
    box.textContent = 'Thanks for the feedback.'
  })
</script>`

const recordTs = `app.post('/api/feedback', (req, res) => {
  const { traceId, vote, comment } = req.body
  if (vote !== 'up' && vote !== 'down') return res.status(400).end()
  mcpd.feedback({
    kind: 'thumbs',
    value: vote,
    traceId,
    comment,
    authorKind: 'end_user',
    authorId: req.user.id, // hashed by mcpd at ingest, like a user id
  })
  res.status(204).end()
})`

const recordPy = `from mcpd import FeedbackKind


@app.post("/api/feedback")
def feedback(body: FeedbackRequest, user: User):
    if body.vote not in ("up", "down"):
        raise HTTPException(400)
    mcpd.feedback(
        FeedbackKind.THUMBS,
        body.vote,
        trace_id=body.trace_id,
        comment=body.comment,
        author_kind="end_user",
        author_id=user.id,  # hashed by mcpd at ingest, like a user id
    )`

const recordCurl = `# Needs a write key when the daemon has keys set.
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4317/v1/api/feedback \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -H "authorization: Bearer $MCPD_WRITE_KEY" \\
  -d "{
    \\"feedback_id\\": \\"\\",
    \\"trace_id\\": \\"$TRACE\\",
    \\"kind\\": \\"thumbs\\",
    \\"value\\": \\"down\\",
    \\"comment\\": \\"Named the wrong depot.\\",
    \\"author_kind\\": \\"end_user\\",
    \\"timestamp\\": \\"$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)\\",
    \\"correlation\\": { \\"project\\": \\"support\\" }
  }"`

const findDown = `# The thumbs-down votes of the last week, newest first, with the run each one judged.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/feedback \\
  --data-urlencode 'project=support' \\
  --data-urlencode 'kind=thumbs' \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-7d' \\
  | jq -r '.feedback[] | select(.value == "down") | [.timestamp, .trace_id, .comment] | @tsv'

# Or every run that has any feedback at all.
curl -s "http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces?project=support&has_feedback=true" \\
  | jq -r '.traces[] | [.trace_id, .name, .status] | @tsv'`
---

<Guide
  slug="collect-user-feedback"
  lede="Put a thumbs-up and a thumbs-down button under each answer. Each vote is stored on the trace that produced the answer, so you can open the exact run a person disliked."
  related={['trace-chat-conversations', 'llm-regression-tests-github-actions', 'read-traces-in-claude-code']}
>
  <p>
    When you finish, every vote in your product is a feedback record in mcpd,
    attached to the run it judges, and you have a query that lists the runs people
    voted down. You need runs in mcpd from your chat or answer endpoint, sent with
    an <a href="/docs/integrating" class="link">mcpd SDK</a>, and a backend that
    serves your front end.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Return the trace id with each answer</h2>
  <p>
    A vote has to name the run it is about. Read the trace id from the root span
    while it is open and send it to the browser with the answer.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="the answer endpoint"
    samples={[
      { lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: answerTs },
      { lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: answerPy },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>2. Add the buttons</h2>
  <p>
    The buttons post the vote to your own backend, never to mcpd. A key in a
    browser is a public key, and mcpd answers no cross-origin browser request
    unless it runs with <code>--cors-origin</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="the buttons" samples={[{ lang: 'typescript', label: 'HTML', shiki: 'html', code: buttons }]} />

  <h2>3. Record the vote from your backend</h2>
  <p>
    Your backend checks the vote and records it with the SDK. The SDK sends it in
    its next batch, through the same door as your spans, with the ingest key it
    already has.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="record the vote"
    samples={[
      { lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: recordTs },
      { lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: recordPy },
      { lang: 'curl', label: 'cURL', shiki: 'bash', code: recordCurl },
    ]}
  />
  <p>A feedback record takes these fields:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td><code>thumbs</code> here. Also <code>rating</code>, <code>correction</code>, <code>tag</code> and <code>comment</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>value</code></td><td><code>up</code> or <code>down</code> for <code>thumbs</code>. mcpd stores what you send, so check it first.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>trace_id</code></td><td>The run the vote is about. Add <code>span_id</code> to judge one step.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>comment</code></td><td>Optional text from the person. It is stored, and swept by retention and erasure.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>author_kind</code></td><td>Who voted, such as <code>end_user</code> or <code>reviewer</code>. The feedback list filters on it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>author_id</code></td><td>Optional. Hashed at ingest with the same key as <code>user_id</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />
  <p>
    Feedback is one of the signals that share the run's trace id, so the vote
    shows in the run next to its spans, logs and scores. Machine scores are a
    separate signal, and mcpd never averages the two together; see
    <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Find the runs people voted down</h2>
  <p>
    The feedback list filters by kind and time window, newest first. Filter the
    value with <code>jq</code>, then open each run by its trace id. The trace list
    takes <code>has_feedback=true</code> for every run with a vote of any kind.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="the runs people disliked" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'cURL', shiki: 'bash', code: findDown }]} />
  <p>
    A run that was voted down is a good test case. The
    <a href="/docs/guides/llm-regression-tests-github-actions" class="link">regression test guide</a>
    turns runs like these into a dataset that runs on every pull request.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Why, and what to do</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>422</code> with <code>missing field `feedback_id`</code> from <code>POST /v1/api/feedback</code></td>
        <td>
          The endpoint takes a whole feedback record. Send <code>feedback_id</code> (an empty string lets
          mcpd make one), <code>timestamp</code> and <code>correlation</code>, as in the cURL example.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>401</code> or <code>403</code> from <code>POST /v1/api/feedback</code></td>
        <td>
          The endpoint needs a key with the write scope. With no <code>--write-key</code> set, the daemon
          accepts its ingest keys there.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The Python SDK logs <code>feedback was dropped</code></td>
        <td>
          The call named no trace id and no span was active. Pass the trace id you returned with the
          answer.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Guide>
